= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write the order-$m$ <principal symbol> as $p_m(x,y;\xi,\eta)=\sum_{|\alpha|=m}a_\alpha(x,y)\xi^{\alpha_1}\eta^{\alpha_2}$. The conormal of the plane is $(1,0)$. Therefore the <non-characteristic hypersurface> hypothesis is exactly
$$
\boxed{a_{(m,0)}(0,y)=p_m(0,y;1,0)\ne0\quad\text{for }|y|<1.}
$$
In particular its value at the origin is nonzero, and continuity provides a uniform positive lower bound for its modulus in a sufficiently small neighborhood. No lower-order coefficient enters this condition.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Repeated <integration by parts> against a compactly supported <test function> gives the bilinear <formal adjoint>
$$
\boxed{P^*v=\sum_{|\alpha|\le m}(-1)^{|\alpha|}\partial^\alpha(a_\alpha v).}
$$
There is no boundary remainder because $v$ has <compact support>. The product rule makes this an order-at-most-$m$ linear differential operator with <real analytic> coefficients. The displayed integral pairing has no complex conjugation, so this is the formal transpose; for a sesquilinear pairing the coefficients would be conjugated.

For uniqueness, suppose $Q$ is the difference of two candidates. Then $\int uQv=0$ for every smooth compactly supported $u$ and $v$, so $Qv=0$ for every such $v$. At an interior point, a <polynomial> times a cutoff can prescribe every <derivative> of $v$ through order $m$ independently. Hence each coefficient of $Q$ is zero. This proves uniqueness as a differential operator, not only uniqueness of its integrated action.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Taken literally, the printed condition is insufficient: the choice of which factor vanishes may depend on the multi-index. For a counterexample already at $m=2$, let $P=\partial_x^2$, let $\Omega=(0,a)\times(-b,b)$ be a small rectangle compactly contained in the unit ball, and choose $\chi\in C_c^\infty((-b,b))$ nonnegative with positive integral. Put
$$
u=x(a-x)\chi(y),\qquad v=1.
$$
On the vertical edges $u=0$, and on the horizontal edges $u$ and its <derivatives> vanish. Every first <derivative> of $v$ is zero. Thus for every $|\beta|\le1$ the same-index alternative in the PDF holds at every boundary point. But
$$
\boxed{\int_\Omega(Pu)v=-2a\int_{-b}^b\chi(y)\,dy\ne0=\int_\Omega u(P^*v).}
$$

The valid <complete boundary-jet condition for formal adjoints> is that, at each boundary point, either all <derivatives> of $u$ through order $m-1$ vanish or all such <derivatives> of $v$ vanish. On a bounded piecewise smooth domain with smooth coefficients up to its closure, repeated <integration by parts> produces boundary terms of the form $(\partial^\gamma u)\partial^\delta(a_\alpha v)$, multiplied by a normal component, with $|\gamma|,|\delta|\le m-1$. Expanding the second factor shows that every summand vanishes under this stronger whole-jet alternative. Hence the desired identity holds under that condition. The later cap argument satisfies this stronger condition face by face, so it remains valid despite the literal error in this subpart. Boundary regularity and finite integrals are also needed for a general noncompact-support integration formula; the caps used below have them.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The order-$m$ <principal symbol> of the <formal adjoint> is $p_m^*=(-1)^mp_m$. Derivatives falling on coefficients contribute only lower-order terms. Thus the original plane remains a <non-characteristic hypersurface>.

For the curved face use the defining function $\rho_\varepsilon=x+y^2-\varepsilon$, whose conormal is $(1,2y)$. On the face with $x>0$, $|y|<\sqrt\varepsilon$ and $0<x\le\varepsilon$. Therefore $(x,y,1,2y)$ tends uniformly to $(0,0,1,0)$ as $\varepsilon\downarrow0$. Since $p_m^*(0,0;1,0)\ne0$, continuity gives
$$
\boxed{|p_m^*(x,y;1,2y)|\ge c>0\quad\text{on every sufficiently small curved face}.}
$$
The same argument supplies a common neighborhood where the transformed coefficient of the highest <normal derivative> is nonzero, permitting the <real analytic> normal form used below.

= e
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The coordinate change has inverse $x=\tilde x-\tilde y^2+\varepsilon$, $y=\tilde y$. Thus the coefficient function needed after changing variables is
$$
\boxed{\tilde f_\varepsilon(\tilde x,\tilde y)=f(\tilde x-\tilde y^2+\varepsilon,\tilde y).}
$$
The PDF's displayed formula has an undefined subscript $f_\alpha$ and writes the forward change in the argument. It is not the coefficient pullback by the stated coordinates; the inverse expression above is the one used in transforming the equation. The forward <polynomial> composition, if intended instead, has the same type of uniform bounds, but is not that pullback.

For $|(\tilde x,\tilde y)|\le1/2$ and $0\le\varepsilon\le1/32$, the inverse image lies in the fixed compact subset specified by $|x|\le25/32$, $|y|\le1/2$, whose Euclidean radius is less than one. A <real analytic function> extends holomorphically to a complex neighborhood of this compact set: local extensions agree on overlaps by uniqueness, and a finite covering gives a common smaller neighborhood. The <polynomial> inverse maps a fixed small complex neighborhood of the closed half-ball into that neighborhood, uniformly in $\varepsilon$. The extensions are bounded there by a common constant $A$. Applying the multivariable <Cauchy estimate> on equal-radius polydiscs gives
$$
\boxed{\|\partial^\beta\tilde f_\varepsilon\|_\infty\le A\beta!R^{-|\beta|}=\gamma\beta!\zeta^{|\beta|}.}
$$
This is <uniform analyticity under polynomial coordinate changes>. The argument gives a compact margin and a common complex radius, not merely separate analyticity for each parameter.

= f
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

In the flattened coordinates, $\partial_x=\partial_{\tilde x}$ and $\partial_y=2\tilde y\partial_{\tilde x}+\partial_{\tilde y}$. The transformed <formal adjoint> consequently has coefficients obtained from finitely many <real analytic> coefficient pullbacks and <polynomial> factors. They have uniform analyticity bounds on a fixed small ball. By the preceding non-characteristic argument, its coefficient of $\partial_{\tilde x}^m$ has modulus bounded away from zero there, uniformly for small $\varepsilon$. Division by it preserves uniform <real analytic> bounds.

The transformed forcing $\tilde g_\varepsilon$ has analogous bounds, with constants that may depend on $g$. Apply the <Cauchy-Kovalevskaya theorem> in normal form with zero normal data through order $m-1$ at $\tilde x=0$. The supplied <majorant series> radius result yields a common solution radius $r_g>0$ for all sufficiently small parameters. Tangential differentiation of zero normal data shows that the entire <boundary jet> through order $m-1$ is zero; changing coordinates preserves this vanishing.

For $(x,y)$ in the closed cap, $|\tilde x|\le\varepsilon$ and $|\tilde y|\le\sqrt\varepsilon$. Choose $\varepsilon$ so small that $\sqrt{\varepsilon^2+\varepsilon}<r_g$, with a strict margin. The whole closed cap then lies in the common <real analytic> solution neighborhood. Pulling the solution back proves
$$
\boxed{P^*v=g\text{ throughout }\omega_\varepsilon,\quad j^{m-1}v|_{\tilde\Sigma_\varepsilon}=0.}
$$
In fact $v$ is <real analytic> on a neighborhood of its closure, which is useful for the boundary integration below.

= g
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=g}

Fix the coefficient normal form and a positive analyticity exponent $\zeta_0$ once and for all. If $g$ is a <polynomial>, its pullback $\tilde g_\varepsilon=g(\tilde x-\tilde y^2+\varepsilon,\tilde y)$ is again a <polynomial>, with degree bounded independently of $\varepsilon$. On a fixed closed coordinate ball, its finitely many nonzero <derivatives> are uniformly bounded as the parameter ranges over a fixed compact small interval. Hence there is a finite $A_g\ge1$ such that
$$
\|\partial^\beta(\tilde g_\varepsilon/A_g)\|_\infty\le\beta!\zeta_0^{|\beta|}\quad\text{for every }\beta\text{ and every small }\varepsilon.
$$
All higher <derivatives> vanish, so arbitrarily high <polynomial> degree causes no failure of this bound; it only changes $A_g$.

The normalized forcing belongs to one common majorant class. The <Cauchy-Kovalevskaya theorem> therefore gives a radius $r_*>0$ independent of $g$. The problem is linear with zero data, so multiplying the normalized solution by $A_g$ solves the original forcing without changing its radius. Choose one $\varepsilon$ with the cap inside this radius. Thus
$$
\boxed{\text{one }\varepsilon>0\text{ works for every polynomial }g.}
$$
This is the <uniform Cauchy radius for polynomial forcing>. It asserts a common domain, not a uniform solution-amplitude bound over all <polynomials>.

= h
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=h}

For each <polynomial> $g$, take the <real analytic> adjoint solution on the common cap from the preceding part. On the flat boundary face the full <boundary jet> of $u$ vanishes; on the curved face that of $v$ vanishes. The <complete boundary-jet condition for formal adjoints>, proved under its corrected hypothesis, therefore applies. The two corner points have zero boundary measure, and the cap is a bounded piecewise smooth domain. It follows that
$$
\int_{\omega_\varepsilon}ug=\int_{\omega_\varepsilon}uP^*v=\int_{\omega_\varepsilon}(Pu)v=0.
$$
By the <Weierstrass approximation theorem>, <polynomials> uniformly approximate $\bar u$ on the compact cap closure. Taking the limit gives $\int_{\omega_\varepsilon}|u|^2=0$. Continuity then gives $u=0$ pointwise in the cap. For real $u$, simply approximate $u$ itself.

A positive cap alone is not a neighborhood of the origin. Apply the same argument after reflecting $x\mapsto-x$; the reflected operator remains <real analytic> and non-characteristic. Shrinking the two parameters to their minimum proves vanishing on
$$
\boxed{\{(x,y):|x|+y^2<\varepsilon_*,\ \varepsilon_*>0\}.}
$$
This set is open and contains the origin, including the flat face where the value data vanish. The argument is the <parabolic-cap proof of Holmgren uniqueness>, and it establishes <Holmgren uniqueness theorem> for nonanalytic $C^m$ solutions here.

